---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/guides/segmentation';
const title = "Shopify email segmentation: write rules that change a decision";
const desc = "Turn purchase history, product preferences and temporary intent into maintainable audience rules.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "What should the first segments be?",
    "answer": "Start from the workflows you operate, then define purchase stage, eligibility and required exclusions. There is no universal mandatory count."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can VIP and lapsed overlap?",
    "answer": "Yes. They describe different properties. Define which action takes priority when both apply."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should every SKU become a segment?",
    "answer": "Only when membership changes a necessary action or analysis. Content rules can handle many presentation differences."
  },
  {
    "question": "What happens when a field is missing?",
    "answer": "Document a fallback or review path. Unknown must not silently mean eligible or false in a safety-critical exclusion."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/guides">All operator guides</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-16"><article class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
    <h2>Start with the action, not a segment count</h2>
    <p>Write what will change: eligibility, timing, offer or content. A separate audience is useful when it controls a different action or provides a necessary reporting boundary. A different product image alone may be better handled by content rules. No order-volume threshold or maximum number of segments establishes maturity.</p>
    <h2>Separate lifecycle from eligibility</h2>
    <p>Define never-purchased, first-purchase and repeat-purchase states using qualifying orders and an explicit refund/cancellation policy. VIP and lapsed are overlays, not mutually exclusive lifecycle stages: a valuable customer can also be overdue. Marketing eligibility is a separate gate. A purchase or a high spending score alone is not marketing permission.</p>
    <h2>A segment contract to keep</h2>
    <p>Record name, owner, business purpose, source fields, identity key, boolean expression, time basis, refresh behavior, missing-data behavior, exclusions, affected flows and expected action. Include a sample of matching and nonmatching records and a retirement condition. Record source-to-sender mapping explicitly: a field in Shopify is not proof that the connector imports it.</p>
    <h2>Proposed tea reminder rule</h2>
    <p>For a proposed loose-leaf reminder, qualify on the relevant product family and fulfilled quantity rather than any order. Select a reorder interval from the store’s observed family-level purchase distribution, then pilot it. Exclude an active subscription that already supplies that family; handle paused, cancelled and unknown states separately. A new unrelated teaware purchase should not reset the tea clock. Missing subscription status should trigger review rather than silently becoming inactive.</p>
    <h2>Proposed pen preference rule</h2>
    <p>A nib-width purchase can guide compatible stock recommendations, but a gift order need not reveal the buyer’s preference. Prefer an explicit preference when available and provide a neutral fallback. Test unknown width, a changed preference, a returned pen and multiple widths. Do not infer compatibility from a broad product category or turn an old preference into permanent exclusion from the catalog.</p>
    <h2>Keep intent temporary</h2>
    <p>Cart, waitlist and recent browse signals have entry and exit conditions independent of lifecycle. Specify what clears the state: purchase of the relevant item, cancellation of the request, expiry or an explicit preference change. A customer can remain a repeat buyer while entering a cart audience. Resolve competing messages through a send-priority policy rather than deleting a valid audience membership.</p>
    <h2>Use RFM only when the rules help</h2>
    <p>Recency, frequency and monetary value can describe purchasing behavior, but thresholds depend on catalog cadence, history coverage and returns. Define the lookback and currency treatment before creating tiers. Compare whether the tiers change a useful action beyond simpler rules. Seasonal launches and subscription renewals can dominate scores; distinguish them before treating a score as a forecast.</p>
    <h2>Test boundaries and maintain dependencies</h2>
    <p>Proposed QA cases: zero, one and two qualifying orders; an order exactly on the cutoff; a late refund; a merged identity; stale subscription state; and an unsubscribed VIP. Compare source records, computed membership and the queued send audience. Test that an exclusion arriving after enrollment is checked again before dispatch. Keep a dependency list before merging or retiring a segment so a dormant seasonal flow is not broken.</p>
    <h2>Evaluate the sender without invented winners</h2>
    <p>Ask Sequenzy and other candidates to express the same nested rule and show both matched and excluded records. Demonstrate null handling, refresh latency, exportable definitions and an ordinary operator edit. Compare dated commercial quotes separately: splitting an existing audience is not itself proof that billable profile count changes. Predictive labels require documented inputs and a useful decision, not an assumed advantage.</p>
    <h2>Data evidence and next steps</h2>
    <p>Shopify’s <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Order">Order reference</a> documents purchase and refund data and the default restriction on older order access. The <a class="link" href="https://shopify.dev/docs/api/admin-graphql/latest/objects/Customer">Customer reference</a> describes history and marketing preferences. Checked November 25, 2026; neither establishes what a particular email connector imports. Confirm history coverage before treating missing records as no purchases.</p>
    <p>Continue with <a class="link" href="/guides/revenue-attribution">revenue measurement</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/segmentation">audience rules</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/selection-framework">tool selection</a>, <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing-models">cost modeling</a>, or the <a class="link" href="/apps">app directory</a>.</p>
  </article></section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
